  [3mBuys:[0m proper encapsulation, no globals, load order handled by the import graph.
  [3mCosts:[0m module scripts are blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m — opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops working, so you need a
  local HTTP server to develop. It also adds a [1mthird[0m module convention to a repo where [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS.
  [3mRules out:[0m casual file-open development.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate (the cost is workflow, not code).

  [1mC. Add a build toolchain[0m
  npm + esbuild/Vite, bundle to [38;5;153mdist/[39m, ES modules throughout, unify [38;5;153msrc/[39m too.
  [3mBuys:[0m the conventional answer; npm deps and a test runner become easy.
  [3mCosts:[0m a build step, a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, and a config to maintain — for a page with one form and two functions. This is
  the tail wagging the dog today.
  [3mRules out:[0m nothing, but it's the hardest to undo.
  [3mComplexity:[0m high.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's honest about the project's current size, it requires no workflow change, and the session module's
  public interface is identical in all three — so the migration to B or C later is a mechanical change to how the file
  is [3mloaded[0m, not to what it [3mdoes[0m. Picking C now buys tooling you don't yet have a use for.

  ---

  One thing I want to flag before you choose, because it may change the shape of the answer: [1munder this design, [38;5;153mlogin[39m [0m
  [1mprobably should not take a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter.[0m A parameter is an [3minput[0m, but the identity is something login [3mestablishes[0m
  — the caller at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m has no ID to pass that it didn't already type into the username box. The natural shape is
  [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m unchanged, with login recording the identity into the session module and the persisted value
  being read by other forms via [38;5;153mAppSession.getIdentity()[39m.

  That's a departure from your literal request, so it's your call, not mine:

